437: ## RG.6. Full comparison with the marked infinity source
438: 
439: This paragraph supplies the punctured-family arrow to the infinity
440: presentation IF.1--6. It uses that presentation's full reference
441: overlap, defined by transport of the global reference at one
442: nonzero epsilon. It does not identify a central collision attachment
443: or infer the characteristic value of its boundary.
444: 
445: The source, section and actual selected height map are
446: \[
447:  v=\epsilon^{-2}-1,\quad
448:  X=\epsilon^{-2}x,\quad Y=\epsilon^{-3}y,\quad
449:  T=\epsilon^{-4}t,\quad
450:  g=\epsilon(1-\epsilon^2),\quad
451:  q_{1,\rm old}=\epsilon^{-4}q_{*,\rm new}.
452:                                                         \tag{RG.12}
453: \]
454: They give exactly
455: \[
456:  y^2=x^3+(-2+3\epsilon^2)x^2+tx+
457:               \epsilon^2(1-\epsilon^2)^2,\quad G=(0,g),
458: \qquad
459:  t_*=(1-\epsilon^2)(1-3\epsilon^2).
460: \]
461: The selected normalization coordinate changes by
462: \(s_{\rm new}=\epsilon s_{\rm old}\). Therefore
463: \[
464:  Z_{\rm old}=\frac{s_{\rm old}-\rho}{s_{\rm old}+\rho}
465:             =\frac{s_{\rm new}-1}{s_{\rm new}+1}
466:             =Z_{\rm new}.
467:                                                         \tag{RG.13}
468: \]
469: The Tate period \(Q\) and marked \(P\) agree literally.
470: The physical-height units satisfy
471: \[
472:  \kappa_{\rm old}=\epsilon^4\kappa_{\rm new},\qquad
473:  P=-\frac{1+\epsilon}{1-\epsilon},\qquad
474:  \left.\kappa_{\rm new}\right|_{q_*=0}
475:                        =-\frac{1-\epsilon^2}{64}.
476: \]
477: Every nonconstant unit in these equations is retained. The
478: integer epsilon power is a toric unit on the punctured parameter
479: annulus, with its winding; the selected fan is unchanged by
480: (RG.13).
481: 
482: The Cartier map is the canonical divisor map, not an identification
483: only of generic Picard classes. The primitive lift satisfies
484: \[
485:  I_\epsilon U_{a,\rm old}I_\epsilon^{-1}
486:                   =\epsilon^{2a}U_{a,\rm new}.
487:                                                         \tag{RG.14}
488: \]
489: Since \(ab=2\), its \(b\)-th power cancels exactly the
490: \(\epsilon^{-4}\) in the selected height. Thus
491: \[
492:  I_\epsilon\bigl(2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda
493:              q_{1,\rm old}U_{a,\rm old}^{\,b}\bigr)
494:  I_\epsilon^{-1}
495:        =2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda q_{*,\rm new}U_{a,\rm new}^{\,b}.
496:                                                         \tag{RG.15}
497: \]
498: The Hom section itself provides a second literal check. With
499: \[
500:  F_{21}=\frac{Y+v+TX/(2v)}X,\qquad
501:  F_{12}=\frac{Y-v-TX/(2v)}X
502: \]
503: in the old presentation, substitution gives
504: \(F_{ab,\rm old}=\epsilon^{-1}F_{ab,\rm new}\) and
505: \(v=\epsilon^{-3}g\). Hence
506: \[
507:        \frac{q_{1,\rm old}}{vF_{ab,\rm old}}
508:                =\frac{q_{*,\rm new}}{gF_{ab,\rm new}}. \tag{RG.16}
509: \]
510: This retains the actual degree-two Cartier correction as well.
511: In particular the scalar in (RG.10) is unchanged.
512: 
513: On the logarithmic epsilon cover, the cap comparison is
514: \[
515:  z_{\rm old}=\epsilon^{4/m}z_{\rm new}.
516:                                                         \tag{RG.17}
517: \]
518: For \(m=4p\), its minimal source coordinates \(X',Y'\) are
519: unchanged. For \(m=4p-1\), the old-to-new arrow multiplies
520: them by \(\epsilon^{-2/m},\epsilon^{-3/m}\). It carries the
521: marked section, source resolution ideals and every Cartier
522: correction by their actual coordinate maps. Its full positive
523: epsilon endpoint is
524: \[
525:  D_{\rm new}^{-4}:\quad
526:  (z,X',Y')\mapsto
527:  (\zeta_m^{-4}z,\zeta_m^{-2}X',\zeta_m^{-3}Y')
528:                                                         \tag{RG.18}
529: \]
530: in the reduced case; the last two factors are one in the good
531: case. The ordinary and cap source arrows commute with the
532: specified finite root maps on each parameter chart.
533: 
534: Here is the completion of that endpoint on the affine line
535: atlas. Write \(A_0=L D\), with \(L\) covering the specified
536: signed division translation. The central finite commutator
537: character is one: in the good root the division point is \(O\);
538: in the reduced root the actual line
539: \(\mathcal O(2G'-O)\) restricts to the trivial line on the
540: compact reduced III fibre. Its commuting source maps have
541: constant line multipliers there. The finite eigenspace
542: average, retaining its actual conjugating unit, therefore
543: gives \(DLD^{-1}=L\). In this actual atlas
544: \[
545:                          A_0^4D^{-4}=L^4.
546:                                                         \tag{RG.19}
547: \]
548: The latter extends and has identity central source translation
549: in both branches; after its central scalar is retained it has
550: the small extending Lie logarithm. For the reduced branch the
551: annular comparison still contains
552: \[
553:                     4\log R-\log(4R)=2(1+i).
554:                                                         \tag{RG.20}
555: \]
556: The signed source part is multiplied by \(n\), and its line
557: part is the actual Cartier factor of automorphy. This entire
558: period is included in the total atlas cochain. It is not
559: replaced by the small logarithm or suppressed in (RG.19).
560: 
561: To specify the annular marking, fix one nonzero
562: \(\epsilon_*\), transport the full global ordinary-to-cap
563: map through (RG.12--17), and use that entire map as the
564: infinity reference \(F_*\). It includes the given integral
565: \(d\delta\) and all finite characters. The cap-overlap source
566: and Cartier family is smooth over the full epsilon disc:
567: its base annulus avoids the unselected collision and the
568: selected fibre. The actual root division branches, norm
569: units and their equivariant line lifts extend on the cap.
570: Their nonzero norm-ratio has the fixed central value \(+2\)
571: or \(-2\), including at \(\epsilon=0\).
572: 
573: Continue \(F_*\) by the parameter-disc exponential lifting
574: on every ordinary and finite groupoid overlap, and solve the
575: remaining vector cochains by the fixed source-base Cauchy
576: contours of RCZ.3 and RCZ.5. At the reference fibre the full
577: maps agree, so every integral overlap and cyclic-group
578: discrepancy is zero there. It is locally constant and hence
579: zero throughout the parameter disc. The nonzero doubled
580: period (RG.20) is kept when changing logarithmic parameter
581: charts; the two compared total cocycles carry the same
582: term. Before taking a Lie logarithm at the reduced root,
583: use the actual suspension power to remove its component
584: translation. Finite averaging is applied to vector
585: cochains, not to lattice components. The root line
586: exponentials extend by the actual resolved Cartier maps.
587: This constructs precisely IF.3's full \(F_0\), with its
588: reference, rather than choosing an unmarked overlap.
589: 
590: For clarity, the scalar closing defect is explicit. Put
591: \[
592:  t_\epsilon=\frac{\log\epsilon}{2\pi i},\qquad
593:  \ell(\epsilon)=\frac{\log(1-\epsilon^2)}{2\pi i}.
594: \]
595: Then \(t_v=-2t_\epsilon+\ell(\epsilon)\).
596: The old and new ordinary forward arrows for the positive
597: epsilon loop are \(q_{1,\rm old}^{-2nk}\) and
598: \(q_{*,\rm new}^{-2nk}\). Their ratio is
599: \(\epsilon^{8nk}\). The obligatory scalar cochain on the
600: logarithmic parameter cover is
601: \[
602:  c_0(t_\epsilon)=
603:        \exp\{-2\pi i\,4nk\,t_\epsilon(t_\epsilon-1)\},
604:  \qquad
605:  \frac{c_0(t_\epsilon+1)}{c_0(t_\epsilon)}
606:                               =\epsilon^{-8nk}.
607:                                                         \tag{RG.21}
608: \]
609: The unit \((1-\epsilon^2)^k\), its actual logarithm and
610: the corresponding cap unit \(h\) remain in the full
611: ordinary-to-cap square. Equation (RG.21) is a cochain in
612: that square, not by itself a single-valued global scalar.
613: The cap correction (RG.19), the source and line maps, and
614: the remaining vector cochain are carried simultaneously.
615: 
616: The two local family comparisons on angular parameter
617: charts now differ by an actual global extending
618: translation. The rational source-section and line
619: valuation argument of RCZ.4 applies to these marked
620: punctured E7 sources and identifies this map with a
621: holomorphic physical scalar. Factor that scalar on the
622: two-chart epsilon annulus by RCZ.4's literal
623: Cauchy--Green solution: for its overlap logarithm
624: \(\ell_c\), set \(b_+=\chi_-\ell_c\),
625: \(b_-=-\chi_+\ell_c\), solve
626: \(\bar\partial v=\bar\partial b_\pm\) using
627: \(1/(\pi(\epsilon-w))\), and use
628: \(e^{b_\pm-v}\). The two adjusted actual maps then glue
629: on both overlap components. This retains the scalar
630: winding, (RG.21), the full finite cocycle and (RG.20).
631: 
632: Thus on the punctured parameter annulus the global
633: infinity restriction is carried to IF's actual source,
634: Cartier, cap and \(F_0\) family with the literal scalar
635: \((\epsilon^{-2}-1)^N\lambda_0\). Its positive return
636: is \(I_{\mathcal T}(-2k)\) in (RG.11), using the inverse
637: closing arrows on both sides. This is an annular family
638: morphism; it asserts neither a central filling nor
639: any equality between the two source-zero fan returns.
640: 
641: Finally the reflected source descriptions match through
642: one actual period. IF's half-turn arrow is
643: \(\mathcal I_\epsilon(Z,V)=(1/Z,P^aV)\).
644: At the target \(P_{\rm target}=P^{-1}\), so
645: \[
646:         h_{\rm target}\circ\mathcal I_\epsilon
647:                   =(Q/Z,V)=\mathcal R_\rho .
648:                                                         \tag{RG.22}
649: \]
650: This is the exact quotient arrow, with its integral
651: height reindexing and all its unit factors. It is the
652: reflection used in (RG.6), rather than an additional
653: unmarked endpoint identification.
